#
# prima-materia — the search of the Agrippa run, 2026-09-22
#
# One search, and it came back empty, which is what it was worth carrying. The
# question is the one the examinability-driven entrance of SPEC §14 puts to
# every corpus it opens, and this corpus answers it in a way that puts it at the
# far end of the holding's range: conditions in quantity, failures in quantity,
# and not one perceptible sign that the thing worked.
#
# In a file of its own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:AgrippaSuccessSignSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the three books name a perceptible sign that an operation has worked"@en, "Ob die drei Buecher ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen dafuer nennen, dass eine Verrichtung gewirkt hat"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Agrippa, De occulta philosophia, the whole of the volume in the English translation of 1651 — the three books, the address to the reader, the two letters and the censure printed after book III (trans. J. F., London 1651)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the operator what he would perceive when an operation of the work has taken effect: an appearance, a light, a warmth, a voice, a state of the body, any observable announced in advance as the mark of success. The work states conditions of success in quantity and conditions of failure in quantity; whether it anywhere states a sign had to be established across the whole volume, because a run that read only the chapters on dignification and on celestial opportunity would have found none and learnt nothing from that."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole volume, case-insensitive, for: thou shalt see, you shall see, shall appear unto, will appear unto, sign that, a sign of, token of, thou shalt know, you shall know, shalt perceive, shall perceive, thou shalt find, shall feel, shall hear, if it be done rightly. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-22" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-22" ;
    skos:note "Not one sign of an operation. The fifteen terms produce seventeen hits in the whole volume and every one of them is something else: the translator's own preface telling the buyer that he shall see the possibility of these things confirmed by reason and example; the chapters on astrological judgement, where what shall appear is a prodigy or a configuration to be interpreted and not the result of anything done; a passage on extracting the name of a genius, where what shall appear is the star that governs a person; and scriptural quotations about seeking and finding and about faith as a grain of mustard seed."@en ;
    skos:note "What the empty result is worth, set against what the same volume does say. This work states that a firm belief is required and that doubt breaks the operation; that a constant credulity works miracles even in false operations; that the operator must be dignified and that many prosecute the art in vain for want of it; that nothing will be done without the assistance of the moon; that neglect of the true motions defrauds many of their desired effect; that publication destroys the experiment; and that a reader who does not obtain his desire should accuse himself. Seven places where a failure is accounted for, and no place where a success is recognised. A transmission arranged that way cannot be wrong at any point its own text admits."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison that makes the count readable rather than impressive. The Greek magical papyrus this holding opened at the same time announces a dozen sights in order and states one forfeiture; this work states seven kinds of failure and no sight. They are opposite arrangements of the same silence, and neither gives the practitioner a criterion: the papyrus tells him what he will see and never what would show it had failed, and this work tells him what will have gone wrong and never what it looks like when nothing has."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a later run should go, and what it must not conclude. The fourth book that circulates under this author's name is reported to supply exactly what is missing here, in the form of the shapes in which the spirits of the planets appear; it is a work of 1559, twenty-four years after his death, it was not opened in this run, and nothing about it is claimed. If it does carry the signs, the interesting fact will be that the pseudepigraph supplies what the authentic work withholds."@en .
